   "text": "# How Hash Maps Handle Collisions\n\nA **hash map** (or hash table) stores key-value pairs by computing a hash of the key to determine an index in an underlying array. A **collision** occurs when two different keys hash to the same index. There are two primary strategies to handle collisions:\n\n## 1. Chaining\nEach bucket in the array holds a **linked list** (or another collection) of entries. When a collision occurs, the new entry is simply appended to the list at that bucket.\n\n- **Pros:** Simple to implement; handles high load factors well.\n- **Cons:** Extra memory for pointers; cache-unfriendly.\n\n## 2. Open Addressing\nWhen a collision occurs, the algorithm **probes** for the next available slot in the array using a probing sequence (linear, quadratic, or double hashing).\n\n- **Pros:** Better cache locality; no extra memory for pointers.\n- **Cons:** Performance degrades",
